<script>
  import Navbar from '../../components/Navbar.pulse';
  import Footer from '../../components/Footer.pulse';

  let title = "Pulse v0.14: The \"Vibe Coding\" Update";
  let subtitle = "Unified Runtime, Microtask Scheduling, and High-Performance Primitives.";
  let date = "February 5, 2026";
</script>

<Navbar />

<main class="page">
  <div class="content">
    <div class="header">
      <span class="date">{date}</span>
      <h1>{title}</h1>
      <p class="subtitle">{subtitle}</p>
    </div>

    <div class="body">
      <p>
        Pulse v0.14 is here, and it's our biggest architectural leap yet. We've focused entirely on 
        "Vibe Coding" performance—ensuring that the framework feels instantaneous even under heavy loads. 
        This release brings a unified runtime, intelligent scheduling, and major optimizations to our core primitives.
      </p>

      <h2>Unified Runtime Architecture</h2>
      <p>
        In previous versions, the runtime logic was scattered across bundler and server directories. 
        v0.14 consolidates everything into a single, optimized <code>src/runtime</code> core. 
        This not only reduces the bundle size but also ensures consistent behavior across client and server environments. 
        We've removed duplicate files and established a "Single Source of Truth" for all framework logic.
      </p>

      <h2>Microtask Scheduler</h2>
      <p>
        State updates are now batched using a <strong>Microtask Scheduler</strong>. 
        Instead of updating the DOM immediately with every signal change (which can cause layout thrashing), 
        Pulse now collects updates and flushes them in a single coherent microtask.
      </p>
      <div class="highlight-box">
        <strong>The Benefit:</strong>
        <code>
// v0.13 (Synchronous - 3 DOM updates)
setCount(1); // update
setCount(2); // update
setCount(3); // update

// v0.14 (Batched - 1 DOM update)
setCount(1);
setCount(2);
setCount(3);
// ... Microtask flushes once with value 3
        </code>
      </div>

      <h2>Optimized DOM Helpers</h2>
      <p>
        We've introduced two powerful new helpers: <code>template()</code> and <code>walk()</code>.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong><code>template()</code></strong>: Creates cached <code>&lt;template&gt;</code> elements once, allowing us to clone nodes efficiently instead of creating them from scratch.</li>
        <li><strong><code>walk()</code></strong>: A highly optimized DOM traverser that uses pre-calculated paths (like a treasure map) to locate dynamic nodes instantly, bypassing slow <code>querySelector</code> calls.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Supercharged Primitives</h2>
      <p>
        The <code>&lt;List&gt;</code> primitive has received a massive upgrade:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Virtualization:</strong> Render massive datasets (1000+ items) with zero lag by using the `virtual` prop. Pulse only renders what's visible on screen.</li>
        <li><strong>FLIP Animations:</strong> Automatic, buttery-smooth 60fps reordering animations for lists using the FLIP (First, Last, Invert, Play) technique.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>v0.13 vs v0.14 Comparison</h2>
      <div class="table-container">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Feature</th>
              <th>v0.13</th>
              <th>v0.14 (Current)</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Runtime Structure</strong></td>
              <td>Fragmented (Bundler/Server split)</td>
              <td><strong>Unified Core</strong></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Reactivity</strong></td>
              <td>Synchronous (Immediate)</td>
              <td><strong>Microtask Batching (Async)</strong></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>DOM Traversal</strong></td>
              <td>Path Walker (Basic)</td>
              <td><strong>Optimized <code>walk()</code> & <code>template()</code></strong></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>List Rendering</strong></td>
              <td>Keyed Reconciliation</td>
              <td><strong>Virtualization & FLIP Animations</strong></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Testing</strong></td>
              <td>Manual</td>
              <td><strong>Bun Test Suite (Core, DOM, Compiler)</strong></td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>Testing Infrastructure</h2>
      <p>
        Reliability is key. We've introduced a comprehensive testing suite using <strong>Bun Test</strong> and <strong>Happy DOM</strong>. 
        We now have automated unit tests covering the Core Reactivity system, DOM Runtime, and the Compiler, ensuring that Pulse assumes nothing and verifies everything.
      </p>

      <h2>What's Next?</h2>
      <p>
        With a solid, high-performance foundation in place, we're turning our sights to advanced Server-Side Rendering (SSR) features and an even richer ecosystem of built-in components.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</main>

<Footer />

